- # Purchase — Browser Use Checkout
- Two CLI commands drive an online purchase end-to-end:
- - `lobstercash purchase explore` — fast, free price discovery. Browser opens, finds the product, calculates total (incl. shipping/tax), and stops. Browser session stays alive.
- - `lobstercash purchase run` — completes the checkout. Reuses the same browser session from `explore`, so the agent picks up exactly where price discovery stopped — no re-search, no re-add-to-cart.
- Always prefer the two-phase flow over calling `purchase run` directly: it lets you size the virtual card to the real price.
- ## Two-phase purchase flow
- ### Phase 1 — Gather info (no command, just conversation)
- Before calling `purchase explore`, check what you already know from the conversation:
- - shipping address
- - contact email
- - phone number (if the merchant might require it)
- - product preferences (size, color, brand, quantity, etc.)
- Only ask the user for fields you don't already have. If the user says a field isn't needed (e.g. "no phone" or "digital product, no address"), believe them and skip it. Never re-ask for info that's already in the conversation.
- ### Phase 2 — Discover the price
- Pack everything into one natural-language `--description` string. The Browser Use agent reads the description verbatim — including the address — and uses it to calculate shipping cost.
-   --description "RHCP enamel pin set from shop.redhotchilipeppers.com. Ship to: Manuel Hidalgo, 123 Main St, New York NY 10001, US. Email: me@example.com. Phone: +1 555 555 0100." \
-   --merchant-country US
- Flags:
- - `--description` (required) — full natural-language request: product, merchant or merchant URL, address, contact, and any user preferences.
- Outcomes:
- - `completed` — product found, price breakdown returned. Look at `result.total.amount`. Note the `exploreId` for phase 4.
- - `needs_user_input` — agent asks one question. Ask the user, then resume:
-   ```bash
-   ```bash
-   lobstercash purchase explore --explore-id <id> --answer "<user reply>"
-   ```
-   ```
-   This can repeat. The browser session stays alive throughout.
- - `running` — long-poll timeout. Re-run the same poll command:
-   lobstercash purchase explore --explore-id <id>
- - `failed` — report the failure and ask how to proceed.
- Share the live view URL with the user when present.
- ### Phase 3 — Create a scoped virtual card
- Size the card to the discovered total: round **up** to the nearest $5 (e.g. $47.23 → $50, $31.75 → $35) so a small price change at checkout doesn't cause a decline. Tell the user the rounded amount and why. Use a short product name as the description.
- For the actual request, approval, and `active`-confirmation flow, follow `references/cards-request.md` (and `references/cards.md` for listing). Don't proceed to phase 4 until the card is `active`.
- ### Phase 4 — Complete the purchase
- Pass `--explore-id` so the server reuses the same Browser Use session. The browser is already on the cart or checkout page from phase 2, so the purchase agent picks up immediately. `--merchant-name` and `--merchant-url` are optional in this mode — the server fills them in from the explore record.
-   --description "RHCP enamel pin set. Ship to: Manuel Hidalgo, 123 Main St, New York NY 10001, US. Email: me@example.com." \
- Use the same `--description` you used in `purchase explore` (or a superset — extra context never hurts). You don't need to add the product URL manually; the server reads it from the explore result.
- Optional flags (same as direct `purchase run`):
- - `--max-total "<amount>"` — maximum total including tax and shipping.
- - `--constraint "<text>"` — repeatable extra constraint.
- - `--shipping-json '{...}'`, `--contact-json '{...}'` — typed context (the description is usually enough).
- - `--submit-policy stop-before-submit` — default; stops at final review instead of placing the order.
- - `--submit-policy submit-if-within-mandate` — only when the user explicitly authorized final submission.
- If the explore session expired between phase 2 and phase 4 (rare), the server falls back to a fresh browser session and uses the discovered product URL to navigate directly. The purchase still works, just slightly slower.
- If you already know the exact price and don't need discovery, you can call `purchase run` directly without `--explore-id`:
- In this mode `--merchant-name`, `--merchant-url`, and `--merchant-country` are required.
- ## Purchase description
- The description is authoritative. Pack everything into one natural-language string:
- ## Required-choice loop
- If the agent reaches a required choice that's not specified, it returns `needs_user_input` and keeps the browser session alive. This can happen during either phase:
- - During `explore`: ambiguous product, missing required address field, etc.
- - During `run`: size, color, paid shipping speed, subscription vs one-time purchase, etc.
- When this happens:
- 1. Ask the user the returned question.
- 2. Include the options/context from the command output.
- 3. Resume the same session:
-    ```bash
-    # During explore:
-    lobstercash purchase explore --explore-id <id> --answer "<user answer>"
-    # During run:
-    lobstercash purchase run --purchase-id <id> --answer "<user answer>"
-    ```
- This can repeat. Never guess required purchase choices.
- ## Polling running sessions
- If a command returns `running`, just re-run the same poll command:
- The browser session stays alive in the background between calls.
- - `running` — Browser Use is still working; poll with the ID.
- - `needs_user_input` — ask the user and resume with `--answer`.
-   - `explore`: price found, ready for phase 3.
-   - `run`: reached the intended stopping point or placed the order if the policy allowed.
- - `failed` — report the failure reason and ask how to proceed.
- Always share the live view URL when present so the user can watch.
+ # Purchase — `purchase explore` and `purchase run` reference
+ Reference for the two CLI commands that drive Browser Use checkout.
+ For the end-to-end purchase flow (when to call which command, how to size a card, how to fork on existing cards), see Section A "Buy something online" in [`SKILL.md`](../SKILL.md). This file is a pure flag and behavior reference.
+ ## `lobstercash purchase explore`
+ Free price-discovery pass. Browser opens, finds the product on the merchant's site, calculates the total (incl. shipping/tax), and parks on the cart. The browser session stays alive so a follow-up `purchase run --explore-id` picks up immediately.
+ ```bash
+   --description "<full natural-language request>" \
+   --merchant-country <XX>
+ ```
+ ### Flags
+ ### Flags
+ - `--description` (required) — full natural-language request: product, merchant or merchant URL, address, contact, and any user preferences. See [Writing a good description](#writing-a-good-description) below.
+ - `--explore-id <id>` — resume an existing session to poll status or supply an answer to a `needs_user_input` prompt.
+ - `--answer "<text>"` — used together with `--explore-id` to answer a `needs_user_input` question.
+ ## `lobstercash purchase run`
+ Runs the automated checkout. Two modes:
+ - **With `--explore-id`** (preferred): reuses the parked browser session from a prior `purchase explore`. `--merchant-name`, `--merchant-url`, and `--merchant-country` are read from the explore record and are not required.
+ - **Without `--explore-id`** (single-phase): see [Single-phase purchase](#single-phase-purchase-no-explore) below — `--merchant-name`, `--merchant-url`, and `--merchant-country` are required.
+   --description "<same description as explore>" \
+ - `--card-id` (required) — the order intent ID of the virtual card to charge. From `lobstercash cards list` (`card-id=...`).
+ - `--explore-id <id>` — reuse a prior `purchase explore` session. When set, merchant flags become optional.
+ - `--description` (required) — same description used in `purchase explore` (or a superset). The server uses it verbatim.
+ - `--max-total "<amount>"` — maximum total including tax and shipping. Decline if the cart exceeds this.
+ - `--constraint "<text>"` — repeatable extra constraint (e.g. `--constraint "no third-party seller"`).
+ - `--shipping-json '{...}'` / `--contact-json '{...}'` — typed shipping/contact context. Usually unnecessary because the description carries this info.
+ - `--submit-policy stop-before-submit` (default) — stop at the final review screen so the user can confirm via the live view URL.
+ - `--submit-policy submit-if-within-mandate` — only when the user has explicitly authorized final submission.
+ - `--merchant-name "<name>"`, `--merchant-url "<https://...>"`, `--merchant-country <XX>` — required only when `--explore-id` is **not** set.
+ - `--purchase-id <id>` — resume an existing run session to poll status or supply an answer.
+ - `--answer "<text>"` — used together with `--purchase-id` to answer a `needs_user_input` question.
+ If you already know the exact price and the canonical merchant URL, you can call `purchase run` directly without `--explore-id`:
+ In this mode `--merchant-name`, `--merchant-url`, and `--merchant-country` are required because there is no prior explore record to read them from.
+ ## Writing a good description
+ The `--description` is authoritative — Browser Use reads it verbatim. Pack everything into one natural-language string:
+ ## Resuming and polling sessions
+ Both commands return a session ID (`exploreId` or `purchaseId`) and may return one of three non-terminal statuses (see [Output statuses](#output-statuses)). The browser session stays alive in the background between calls.
+ To **answer a `needs_user_input` prompt**, ask the user the returned question (and any options), then resume the same session with `--answer`:
+ # During explore:
+ lobstercash purchase explore --explore-id <id> --answer "<user answer>"
+ # During run:
+ lobstercash purchase run --purchase-id <id> --answer "<user answer>"
+ Required-choice prompts can repeat. Never invent answers.
+ To **poll a `running` session**, re-run the command with the ID and no `--answer`:
+ - `running` — Browser Use is still working. Re-run the command with the session ID to poll.
+ - `needs_user_input` — agent reached a required choice it cannot make on its own (size, paid shipping, missing field, etc.). Ask the user and resume with `--answer`.
+   - `explore`: price found and the browser is parked on the cart, ready to be reused by `purchase run --explore-id`.
+   - `run`: reached the configured stopping point, or placed the order if `--submit-policy submit-if-within-mandate` was used.
+ - `failed` — report the failure reason and ask the user how to proceed.
+ ## Explore session expiry fallback
+ If the explore session expires between phases (rare), `purchase run --explore-id` falls back to a fresh browser session and uses the discovered product URL from the explore record to navigate directly. The purchase still works, just slightly slower.